# ── Point Configuration ──────────────────────────────────────────────
# This file is optional — docker compose up -d works without it.
# To customize, copy to .env and uncomment the values you want to change.

# container name
# NAME=point

# Port the app listens on inside the container (default: 8000).
# PORT=8000

# Host port to publish — maps to PORT inside the container.
# APP_PORT=8000

# Where to store your blog data (DB, photos, backups). Default: ./data
# DATA_PATH=./data

# Path to your existing photo library (mounted read-only for import)
# PHOTO_LIBRARY_PATH=/home/yourname/Photos

# Override the auto-generated secret key (optional — one is generated automatically)

# Enable AI photo analysis with Google Gemini (optional)
# GEMINI_API_KEY=your-gemini-api-key

# Log level: DEBUG, INFO, WARN, ERROR (default: INFO)
# LOG_LEVEL=INFO

# Comments on posts via the bundled remark42 engine (optional).
# Set BOTH values, `docker compose up -d`, then enable the "Comments" plugin
# in Admin → Plugins. Moderate from Admin → Comments.
# REMARK_URL is your blog's public URL + /comments; REMARK_SECRET is any long
# random string (e.g. `openssl rand -hex 32`).
# REMARK_URL=https://blog.example.com/comments
# REMARK_SECRET=

# Outgoing email over SMTP (optional): password reset + comment email login.
# Works with any SMTP relay — Mailgun (smtp.mailgun.org), Brevo
# (smtp-relay.brevo.com), SendGrid (smtp.sendgrid.net), Amazon SES — or your
# own private SMTP server. Port 465 = implicit TLS, 587 = STARTTLS (automatic).
# Password reset also needs APP_URL (your blog's public URL) to build the link.
# SMTP_HOST=smtp.mailgun.org
# SMTP_PORT=587
# SMTP_USERNAME=postmaster@your-domain.com
# SMTP_PASSWORD=
# SMTP_FROM=blog@your-domain.com
# APP_URL=https://blog.example.com

# Networks whose X-Forwarded-For header may be believed, as a comma-separated
# list of CIDR ranges. Only needed when the proxy or CDN in front of Point
# answers from a public address (e.g. Cloudflare pointed straight at this
# container) — a reverse proxy on this host or this Docker network is trusted
# already. Without it, everyone arriving through one edge shares a rate-limit
# bucket and shows up in your session list as the edge's address.
# Paste your provider's published ranges; a single address needs a /32. Careful:
# anything inside these ranges is trusted to name the client, so 0.0.0.0/0 hands
# that to the world.
# TRUSTED_PROXIES=173.245.48.0/20,103.21.244.0/22,2400:cb00::/32

# Extra HTML injected into <head> on public pages only (analytics,
# verification tags) — never sent to /light or authenticated requests.
# If the snippet loads a script from another origin, also add that origin to
# CSP_SCRIPT_SRC (or CSP_CONNECT_SRC for XHR/fetch/beacon targets).
# HEAD_HTML=<script>/* ... */</script>
# CSP_SCRIPT_SRC=https://analytics.example.com
# CSP_CONNECT_SRC=https://analytics.example.com
